er ticket portal. (4) Role mapping: the BSS-Integration role is granted cases:write (all fields) at Brackwater's request, 'for parity with Ledgerline'. (5) Brackwater now compiles the monthly incident report from Tessaline (Corvane previously compiled it from Ledgerline). Risk rating: medium. Approved: M. Lindqvist.\n- Interview notes, Ilse Varga, senior security engineer (Corvane), 12 Nov (staff-account): 'Alert volume feels about the same as before; the Brackwater analysts are as busy as ever.' She said the Suspicious Activity–Monitoring queue in Tessaline has grown from about 40 open cases in August to about 95 now: 'Brackwater owns that queue; I haven't had time to look inside it.' She is uneasy that 28 rules were dropped in the migration. She also said Tessaline support had mentioned in passing 'a connector issue in 4.1.x that can mess with case categories'. She did not know whether it applied to Corvane.\n- Tessaline SOC dashboard export, Corvane tenant, generated 11 Nov (telemetry): Alerts ingested: Sep 2,884; Oct 3,012 (Ledgerline Mar–Jul average 2,950). Alerts auto-closed by triage playbooks: Sep 1,912; Oct 1,980. Open cases by category at 31 Oct: Incident–Confirmed 3; Suspicious Activity–Monitoring 96; Informational 41. Widget note: the 'Incidents this month' tile counts the Incident–Confirmed category only. Mean-time-to-contain tile: Sep 1h52m, Oct 1h47m.\n\nBRIEF:\nThe fall in incidents is not real. The audit trail shows about 37 cases a month still reached Incident–Confirmed after the migration, which matches the old level of about 38. Of those, 47 over two months were moved to \"Suspicious Activity–Monitoring\" through the Brackwater integration account. Each move came 3h31m–3h42m after the case opened, just after the 4-hour SLA warning flag. The API log ties the moves to a script on a Brackwater analyst jump host that polls for flagged cases every five minutes. The connector itself wrote no categories, so a platform fault is unlikely. Moved cases drop